      ],
      "why": "Only proven-feasible family in the fleet (38/38 of my feasible evals, 13/13 merged-front points, best feasible area 42248 um^2). It is also the only family that can plausibly attack the min-area objective: accuracy is family-independent, so area is driven purely by word width, iteration count and the number of full-width pipeline register banks (one per micro-rotation). Ranges are pulled DOWN versus a generic sweep (W 13..19, N 8..22) because the objective is min area and the accuracy floor is only 2^-12; the search needs room to find the cheapest W/N/guard combination that still clears max_abs_err <= 2.44e-4, plus a little headroom above 12 accuracy_bits for hypervolume spread. Throughput >= 32 msps is never binding here (1 result/cycle)."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 19, "low": 13, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 6, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
      ],
      "why": "Structural upside for a min-area objective: it still retires 1 result/cycle, but registers only every m stages, so the N+2 full-width register banks that dominate a deep pipeline are cut to ceil(N/m)+2. Accuracy is identical to pipelined at the same (W, N, guards), so the whole question is whether it can hold enough Fmax to satisfy sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 us: a 32-request burst is ~31 issue cycles plus latency, so at 100 MHz that is ~0.4 us and passes, while a slow large-m variant (e.g. 60 MHz -> >0.5 us) fails. That explains the fleet's 0/24 feasible so far (24 evals is too few and the m range was untested). m is capped at 2..6 rather than 2..8 so eval budget is not spent on m values whose Fmax almost certainly breaks the batch bound; data_width/n_iter/guards mirror the pipelined grid so the two families can be compared at identical accuracy points."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Constraints first: throughput_msps >= 32 is non-binding for both 1-result/cycle families (Fmax alone is hundreds of msps); max_abs_err <= 2.44e-4 is family-independent and depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding; sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 us is the real architectural filter, because 32 burst requests at 1/cycle means ~31 cycles + latency must fit in 500 ns, i.e. roughly Fmax >= 80-100 MHz depending on m. Objective is min area_um2 with HV ref 100000 against accuracy_bits ref 12, so hypervolume is best served by the cheapest possible designs that sit just above 12 accuracy bits, plus a spread of accuracy_bits at low area. I therefore spend 60% on pipelined (the proven family carrying the entire current front) with ranges deliberately biased to small W and small N to push the area corner, and 40% on pipelined_m, which trades Fmax for a large register-count reduction - the single biggest area lever available to a 1-result/cycle pipeline - with m bounded to 2..6 so the batch-timing bound stays reachable. pipelined_m is a hedge rather than a certainty: if it again yields nothing feasible, the remaining budget in later rounds goes back to refining the pipelined area corner and extending accuracy_bits spread; if it yields feasible small-area points they should enter the merged front directly. Because the L1 system bound is optimistic and the shortlist is re-simulated at L2, I avoid designs that only just clear 0.5 us by keeping the m range and the low-W bias the same across both families, so any surviving pipelined_m point sits on the same accuracy grid as the pipelined points and can be re-selected safely."
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "rationale": "The feasible front is entirely `pipelined` (48/80 feasible; `pipelined_m` 0/40 feasible, best throughput only 78.8 MSPS so it cannot clear the 32-request burst bound). The front is real but only partially covers the objective ranges: area_um2 spans 38698..79654 against the HV reference 100000, and data_width on the front is 18..22 against the registry 8..28, with n_iter only up to 22 of 30. So the high-accuracy / higher-area corner of the HV box (area 79654..100000, accuracy_bits > 17.8) is essentially unmapped, and the low-area end sits right at the accuracy constraint (cheapest design 38698 um^2 at 12.3 bits vs the 2^-12 limit), leaving room to probe cheaper data_width=17 designs. With 80 of 200 evals and 2 rounds left, the highest-value use of the budget is to let NSGA-II map the full `pipelined` ranges (data_width 8..28, n_iter 4..30, angle_guard -2..4, frac_guard 0..4, rounding both) seeded with the current front, rather than narrowing around a region that already looks well-sampled. This directly targets hypervolume coverage, which is the scored objective, while the min-area selection rule is already served by the 38698 um^2 corner.",
  "next_plan": null
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "Budget is exhausted (200/200 evals) and this is the final round, so only stop/infeasible are available. The spec is clearly feasible: 106 of 200 evaluations satisfy all constraints, and the feasible Pareto front holds 25 designs with hypervolume 2.605e+05 (+14.6% this round). Every constraint is met with margin by at least one design — throughput best 171 MSPS vs the 32 MSPS floor, max_abs_err best 6.09e-08 (2^-23.97) vs the 2^-12 limit, and sys_p99_batch_us best 0.251 us vs the 0.5 us bound. The selection rule (min area_um2) is already served by the front's cheapest point, pipelined [data_width=18 n_iter=14 angle_guard=0 frac_guard=0 rounding=round] at area_um2=38698 with accuracy_bits=12.3 (max_abs_err 2^-12.28, still inside the 2^-12 error constraint) and throughput 143 MSPS. The front also spans accuracy_bits 12.3..18.2 and area 38698..77081 um^2, so the trade-off curve is covered well beyond the selected corner. pipelined_m contributed 0 feasible designs (max throughput 78.8 MSPS, best accuracy 15.33 bits), so no unexplored family can improve the front. No further gains are expected within the budget; stop."
  ,"next_plan": null
}
